  "summary": "Optimistic eco-futurism: Art Nouveau-inflected green cities, community solar, hanging gardens and hopeful solarpowered everyday life.",
  "description": "Coined around 2008 and canonized by the Solarpunk anthology (2014) and Adam Flynn's \"Solarpunk: Notes toward a manifesto\", the movement imagines achieved sustainability: vertical gardens, stained-glass solar panels, transit canopies, repair culture and commons governance. Visually it marries Art Nouveau organicism, Arts-and-Crafts honesty and vernacular futurism — the deliberate counter-vision to cyberpunk's decay. Communities (Weeds? — no; Aesthetic: r/solarpunk) treat it as praxis: build it now.",
  "culturalContext": "In 2008, Solarpunk was coined in a blog called \"From Steampunk to Solarpunk\" by John Robert, detailing Steampunks philosophy but with practicality and ethics. Matt Stagg's \"GreenPunk Manifesto\" continued this philosophy adding ethical use of technology with ecological progress. But the aesthetic fully gained traction in 2014 with user Olivia Louise creating a concept art relating to aesthetic. Later Adam Flyn contributed to the genre's rise with Project Hieroglyph, as well as A Solarpunk Manifesto, a comprehensive list of elements in the genre which was published in 2019. In 2021, Chobani released an ad titled \"Dear Alice\" that further cemented the aesthetics' visuals, using motifs such as open fields, a connection with nature, renewable energy and advanced technology.",
